Subject: KeyMint: take the root key of key blobs from the DSA host

The nonsecure KeyMint TA wraps every key blob under a fixed all-zero root key
(soft::Keys::root_kek), so a copy of the device's disk gives up every key on it. DSA's host
gives its guest a root key of its own once per boot; the guest's control daemon leaves it on
tmpfs as /dev/dsa_keymint/root_key, one line: "key <64 lowercase hex digits>" or
"none <reason>".

ta/host_key.rs (HostKeys) reads that file the first time the TA needs a root key, waiting for
it at most 30 seconds from the start of the service, and keeps the result for the life of the
process. A file in any other form counts as no key. With the host's key:

- new blobs are wrapped under it and carry the KEK context "dsa-host-root-key-v1";
- a blob with that context opens only under the host's key, and fails in a boot without one;
- a blob with an empty context (made under the built-in key) still opens, and is reported
  outdated (kek_context_is_outdated), so the TA answers KEY_REQUIRES_UPGRADE and upgradeKey
  wraps it again under the host's key. Keystore and vold upgrade every blob they use on that
  answer, so an existing install keeps all its keys.

Without it the TA behaves exactly as before: built-in key, empty context, no upgrades. The key
agreement key and the unique ID key stay soft::Keys'.

Android.bp builds the same service as dsa-keymint, with its Rust crates linked in, which DSA's
image installs as /vendor/bin/hw/dsa-keymint in place of the
com.android.hardware.keymint.rust_nonsecure APEX: the vendor partition has none of the Rust
shared libraries the APEX carries. The APEX module is left out, since the reduced tree DSA
builds in has neither its key nor its certificate. libkmr_ta_nonsecure takes libzeroize, to wipe
the file contents it reads.

Applies to hardware/interfaces at 0162af698935100a590b7359581ac8b1b80693e5 (Android 17).
